Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action period adventure drama, naturalistic human, canine, wilderness, snow, and fabric detail.

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - DAY

Buck, a massive four-year-old dog with a thick red-brown coat, white chest blaze, dark muzzle, and powerful St. Bernard-and-shepherd build, races across sunlit lawns.

(SOUND: splashing water, barking terriers, cicadas in orchard trees)

Buck carries a laughing grandson on his back beside the cement swimming tank, then settles at JUDGE MILLER'S boots on the veranda.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck lived at Judge Miller's place, in the sun-kissed Santa Clara Valley.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here he was king--king over all creeping, crawling, flying things, humans included.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

MANUEL leads Buck from the dark orchard. A STRANGER waits beneath a sputtering station lamp.

(SOUND: distant locomotive whistle, coins chinking, dry leaves stirring)

The stranger loops stout rope beneath Buck's collar. Buck's proud head lifts; then the rope cinches tight.

STRANGER
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck lunges. The stranger throws him onto his back and chokes him unconscious as a train arrives.

(SOUND: train brakes shrieking, Buck's strangled growl cut short)

INT. SAN FRANCISCO WATERFRONT SHED - NIGHT

Buck lies inside a narrow crate, his coat dirty and his tongue swollen. The SALOON-KEEPER peers through the bars with a tallow candle.

(SOUND: harbor foghorn, crate wood rattling, waves against pilings)

Buck rises whenever the shed door opens, hoping for Judge Miller; each time, he sees only the saloon-keeper.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He could not understand what it all meant.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he felt oppressed by the vague sense of impending calamity.

INT. SEATTLE DOG-BREAKING YARD - DAY

A high-walled yard. A stout MAN IN THE RED SWEATER pries apart Buck's crate with a hatchet.

(SOUND: hatchet chopping crate slats, dogs barking beyond the wall)

Buck explodes through the opening, red-eyed, foaming, and wild. The man calmly swings a heavy club.

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

The club smashes Buck to the ground. Buck charges again and again; each blow stops him.

(SOUND: club striking fur and bone, Buck's roar, men shouting from atop the wall)

Blood flecks Buck's once-glorious coat. At last he collapses, stunned but watching.

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
We've had our little ruction.

MAN IN THE RED SWEATER
Be a good dog and all'll go well. Be a bad dog, and I'll whale the stuffin' outa you.

The man offers water and raw meat. Buck drinks, then takes the meat from his hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was beaten, but he was not broken.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed.

EXT. DYE A BEACH - DAY

Snow falls over a crowded northern beach. Buck steps from the Narwhal and sinks into white slush.

(SOUND: ship bell, gulls crying, snow crunching under paws)

Buck sniffs the snow, licks it, recoils at its cold bite. Nearby, CURLY, a gentle black Newfoundland, approaches a wolfish husky.

The husky strikes once, ripping Curly's face. A silent ring of huskies closes around her.

(SOUND: snarling dogs, Curly's scream, FRANC OIS'S axe chopping snow)

FRANCOIS, a broad, black-faced French-Canadian driver, and small, swarthy PERRAULT beat the pack back with clubs. Curly lies still in bloody snow.

Buck sees SPITZ, a large snow-white husky with narrow eyes, lick his lips.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Once down, that was the end of you.

Buck fixes on Spitz.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
From that moment Buck hated him with a bitter and deathless hatred.

EXT. DYE A BEACH CAMP - NIGHT

Buck, now wearing harness, drags a sled of firewood beside DAVE, a morose dark husky, and SOL-LEKS, a lean one-eyed husky with a scarred face.

(SOUND: harness leather creaking, runners scraping snow, Francois's whip cracking)

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell. I tich heem queek as anyt'ing.

Buck tries to enter the warm tent. Perrault and Francois drive him out with cooking tins.

(SOUND: tin pans clattering, bitter wind across the camp)

Buck shivers in open snow. He finds BILLEE, a small soft-coated husky, curled inside a snow hollow.

Buck digs his own hollow and burrows beneath the snow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Another lesson. So that was the way they did it.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Buck, visibly leaner but hard-muscled beneath his thick coat, pulls in the traces behind Spitz's lead. Frozen river and white hills stretch ahead.

(SOUND: sled runners hissing, dog bells jingling, wind sweeping the ice)

Buck learns to answer “mush” and “ho,” to swing wide on bends, and to keep clear of Dave when the sled descends.

PERRAULT
T'ree vair' good dogs.

FRANCOIS
Dat Buck, heem pool lak hell.

At camp, Buck gulps his fish before the other dogs can steal it. He watches PIKE silently steal bacon, then steals a whole chunk while DUB is blamed.

(SOUND: dogs snapping over food, Perrault's angry shout, firewood popping)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Under the law of club and fang, whoso took such things into account was a fool.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

A blizzard drives snow sideways across the frozen lake. Buck returns to his carefully dug nest and finds Spitz occupying it.

(SOUND: gale whipping canvas, dogs growling, lake ice groaning)

Buck and Spitz circle, teeth bared. Before they strike, starving huskies pour from the dark and raid the camp.

(SOUND: food box overturning, frantic snarls, clubs thudding)

Buck battles three attackers. Spitz slashes Buck's throat from the side, then slips away as Perrault and Francois force the raiders back.

Buck stands bleeding in the snow, staring after Spitz.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
From then on it was war between them.

EXT. FROZEN CREEK - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit flashes through moonlit powder. Buck leads sixty dogs in full pursuit, his red-brown coat streaming over the snow.

(SOUND: pounding paws, wild canine cries, breath roaring)

Spitz cuts across a bend and kills the rabbit. Buck crashes shoulder-first into him.

The pack forms a silent circle around the two rivals.

(SOUND: fangs clicking, low growls, wind falling still)

Buck and Spitz whirl and slash. Spitz opens Buck's shoulder; Buck feints for the throat, then snaps low and breaks Spitz's left foreleg.

(SOUND: bone crunching, Spitz's sharp yelp, dogs panting)

Spitz staggers on three legs. Buck breaks the second foreleg, then drives him into the waiting circle.

The pack closes around Spitz. Buck turns away, bloodied and standing tall.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the successful champion.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The dominant primordial beast had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. YUKON TRAIL - DAY

At dawn, wounded Buck takes the lead position. Francois tries to place Sol-leks before him.

(SOUND: harness buckles clinking, impatient dogs whining, river ice cracking)

Buck blocks Sol-leks and keeps beyond Francois's raised club.

FRANCOIS
Go 'way, Chook!

Buck refuses to move. Perrault watches the wasted hour.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots into the lead traces at once.

(SOUND: sled runners breaking free, Francois laughing in disbelief)

FRANCOIS
Nevaire such a dog as dat Buck!

Buck drives the team in unbroken rhythm over the trail.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL CAMP - NIGHT

Buck leads a heavily laden mail team into camp. His coat is rough, ribs faintly visible, but his eyes remain bright.

(SOUND: tired bells fading, exhausted dogs dropping into snow, fire crackling)

DAVE falls in the traces and cries out. The SCOTCH HALF-BREED examines him by firelight, finding no broken bone.

The next day, Dave bites through Sol-leks's traces and takes his old wheel position.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The pride of trace and trail was his.

EXT. MAIL TRAIL - MORNING

Dave lies unable to rise. He drags himself inch by inch toward the harnessed team.

(SOUND: Dave's thin howl, whip snaps, sled runners grinding away)

The Scotch Half-Breed walks back through the river timber. The team waits, heads lowered.

(SOUND: a single revolver shot beyond the trees)

The man returns without Dave. Buck looks back until the trees hide the place.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck knew, and every dog knew, what had taken place behind the belt of river trees.

EXT. WHITE RIVER TRAIL - DAY

Buck is skeletal now: limp coat, jutting ribs, dried blood in his fur. HAL beats him beside an overloaded sled while CHARLES watches and MERCEDES weeps.

(SOUND: whip lashes, melting ice dripping, Mercedes sobbing)

JOHN THORNTON, a weathered man with dark beard, wool shirt, and fur cap, whittles an axe handle at his campfire.

HAL
Get up there, Buck! Mush on!

Buck does not move. Hal raises a club.

Thornton crosses the snow and hurls Hal away from Buck.

(SOUND: Hal crashing into snow, axe handle striking Hal's knife hand)

JOHN THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

Hal's group drives the remaining team onto rotten river ice. Thornton cuts Buck free and kneels beside him.

(SOUND: harness straps severing, Buck's ragged breathing, ice groaning)

The sled moves away. A broad section of ice breaks; dogs, sled, and people vanish into black water.

(SOUND: ice splitting like thunder, Mercedes's scream swallowed by the river)

Thornton rests a rough hand on Buck's head.

JOHN THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

EXT. JOHN THORNTON'S RIVER CAMP - DAY

Weeks later, Buck is restored: broad-chested, red-brown coat glossy again, white blaze bright against his fur. SKEET, a little Irish setter, cleans his healed shoulder.

(SOUND: river running free, birdsong, campfire crackling)

NIG, a huge black half-bloodhound, play-bows. Buck rolls with both dogs while Thornton laughs.

JOHN THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

Buck takes Thornton's hand gently in his jaws, leaving the print of his teeth.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time.

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

Thornton poles a narrow boat through rapids while HANS and PETE hold a rope along the bank. Buck races anxiously beside the torrent.

(SOUND: rapids roaring, rope straining, Thornton shouting)

The boat flips. Thornton is swept toward jagged rocks. Buck plunges into the current.

Buck reaches Thornton. Thornton grabs Buck's tail, but the current tears them apart.

JOHN THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Hans fastens the rescue rope across Buck's shoulders and launches him again.

(SOUND: rope snapping taut, Buck gasping underwater, Hans yelling)

Buck swims precisely above Thornton, turns, and strikes him like a battering ram. Thornton locks both arms around Buck's neck.

(SOUND: bodies smashing against rocks, Skeet howling from shore)

Hans hauls them to shore. Thornton crawls to Buck's limp body.

JOHN THORNTON
That settles it. We camp right here.

EXT. DAWSON STREET - DAY

A thousand-pound sled of flour sits frozen to hard-packed snow. Fur-clad miners form a ring around it. Buck stands in harness beside Thornton.

(SOUND: crowd murmuring, cold wind, harness chains clinking)

MATTHEWSON slams a sack of gold dust onto a barrel.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

JOHN THORNTON
Buck can start a thousand pounds.

Thornton kneels, placing his face against Buck's head.

JOHN THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck presses Thornton's mittened hand between his teeth.

(SOUND: Buck's restrained whine, crowd falling silent)

JOHN THORNTON
Gee!

Buck twists right. The runners crack loose.

JOHN THORNTON
Haw!

Buck twists left. The sled pivots free.

(SOUND: frozen runners snapping, men drawing breath)

JOHN THORNTON
Now, mush!

Buck throws his full weight forward. His claws score the snow; the sled jerks, then rolls steadily past the hundred-yard marker.

(SOUND: roaring cheers, hats and mittens striking snow, Thornton calling Buck)

Thornton falls to his knees, shaking Buck's head between his hands.

JOHN THORNTON
You can go to hell, sir. It's the best I can do for you, sir.

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - DAY

A spruce-bough lodge stands near piled moose-hide sacks of gold dust. Buck, huge and magnificent, lies beside Thornton's fire as HANS and PETE wash gravel.

(SOUND: sluice water rushing, gold pans scraping, distant raven calls)

Buck lifts his head toward the forest. A long wolf howl drifts from beyond the trees.

(SOUND: distant wolf howl, firewood settling in the flames)

EXT. FOREST CLEARING - NIGHT

Buck stalks into moonlight. A long, lean TIMBER WOLF stands on its haunches and howls.

(SOUND: nocturnal insects, soft paws on moss, sustained wolf howl)

The wolf flees. Buck follows, corners him in a creek bed, then offers his nose instead of his teeth.

They touch noses and run together through the dark forest.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck knew he was at last answering the call.

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - DAY

Buck returns to camp after days away. He moves silently through the trees, his mane raised and nose working.

(SOUND: uncanny forest silence, a single frightened squirrel chattering)

Buck finds Nig dead in brush, an arrow through his body. He finds Hans in the clearing, feathered with arrows.

At the wrecked lodge, YEEHAT HUNTERS chant and dance among scattered gear.

(SOUND: Yeehat chant, arrows rattling, Buck's rising growl)

Buck erupts into the clearing, a blur of red-brown fur and white teeth. He tears down the first hunter and scatters the others into the trees.

(SOUND: terrified cries, arrows whistling, feet crashing through brush)

Buck finds Pete dead in his blankets. At the edge of a muddy pool, Skeet lies with her forepaws in the water.

Buck follows Thornton's scent into the pool. No scent leads out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken.

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - NIGHT

Under a full moon, Buck stands beside the pool. A wolf pack pours into the clearing.

(SOUND: wolves yelping in chorus, wind moving through spruce branches)

The boldest wolf attacks. Buck breaks its neck and faces the pack from a gravel-bank corner.

(SOUND: snapping jaws, claws scraping gravel, wolves snarling)

After a long standoff, the TIMBER WOLF approaches and touches noses with Buck. An old scarred wolf lifts his muzzle and howls.

(SOUND: one ancient wolf howl answered by the gathering pack)

Buck raises his head. His howl joins theirs--deep, long, and unmistakably wild.

The wolves turn into the moonlit forest. Buck runs at their head, beside his wild brother.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He sings a song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

FADE OUT.

THE END
